<article class="env-lemma" id="08D1">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/08D1">Lemma <span data-tag="08D1">36.9.3</span>.</a> In Situation <a href="/tag/08CZ" data-tag="08CZ">36.9.1</a>. Let $M^\bullet $ be a complex of $A$-modules and denote $\mathcal{F}^\bullet $ the associated complex of $\mathcal{O}_ X$-modules. Then there is a canonical isomorphism of complexes </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \mathop{\mathrm{colim}}\nolimits _ e \mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits ^\bullet (I^\bullet (f_1^ e, \ldots , f_ r^ e), M^\bullet ) \longrightarrow \text{Tot}(\check{\mathcal{C}}_{alt}^\bullet (\mathcal{U}, \mathcal{F}^\bullet ))  \]
</div>
  <p> functorial in $M^\bullet $. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Consider the double complex $F^{\bullet , \bullet }$ with terms $F^{p, q} = \mathcal{C}_{alt}^ p(\mathcal{U}, \mathcal{F}^ q)$ discussed in Cohomology, Section <a href="/tag/01FP" data-tag="01FP">20.25</a>. Consider the double complex $G^{\bullet , \bullet }$ with terms $G^{p, q} = \mathop{\mathrm{colim}}\nolimits _ e \mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits _ A(I^{-p}(f_1^ e, \ldots , f_ r^ e), M^ q)$ and differentials given by functoriality (without the intervention of signs). The maps $\psi ^{p, q} : G^{p, q} \to F^{p, q}$ constructed in the proof of Lemma <a href="/tag/08D0" data-tag="08D0">36.9.2</a> are isomorphisms and compatible with the differentials $d_1$ (by the lemma) and $d_2$ (this is clear). However, the differentials $d$ on the complexes on the left and right hand side of the arrow in the lemma have different signs. Namely, for $g \in G^{p, q}$ is given by 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  d(g) = d_2(g) - (-1)^{p + q} d_1(g)  \]
</div>
    <p>
       (see More on Algebra, Section <a href="/tag/0A8H" data-tag="0A8H">15.73</a>) and the differential for $f \in F^{p, q}$ is given by 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  d(f) = d_1(f) + (-1)^ p d_2(f)  \]
</div>
    <p>
       Thus we can fix the signs by multiplying $\psi ^{p, q}$ by $(-1)^{pq + p(p - 1)/2}$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>